Three regulatory packages land within the same twelve-month window: expanded climate and supply-chain disclosure, a tightened director liability regime, and revised capital adequacy treatment for intangible assets. Individually each is manageable. Together they compress the time boards have to re-plan.

## Where Valuations Feel It First

Disclosure obligations raise the fixed cost of being public, and liability changes raise the cost of being wrong. Both land directly in the discount rate investors apply. Our modelling across 60 listed issuers shows the largest valuation sensitivity in mid-cap industrials with complex cross-border supply chains.

{% quote quote="Regulation rarely destroys value on its own. Late preparation does." cite="— Arthur Sterling, Senior Partner" /%}

## A 90-Day Board Agenda

Map every new obligation to a named owner, re-run scenario plans against the revised capital rules, and brief the audit committee on the liability regime before the next reporting cycle. Boards that sequence the work now avoid paying for it twice.
